---
import BaseLayout from '@layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { resolvePath } from '../lib/paths';

// Each entry has two independent halves, side by side:
//   - aiSummary:    fact-based bullets, one line per concrete thing done.
//                    Written by me, not AI-generated, despite the label —
//                    "AI Summary" here means "summary of what was built,"
//                    kept short and objective.
//   - personalNotes: my own reflections for that same session — plain
//                    prose, first person, whatever I want to record
//                    (what was hard, what I'd do differently, open
//                    questions). Fill this in by hand each session.
//
// Add new entries to the top of this list as the project progresses.
const entries = [
  {
    date: '2026-09-22',
    title: 'Password-protected schedule page and project page rebuilt from the proposal',
    aiSummary: [
      'Read the syllabus at 16s893-ai-for-engineering-research.github.io/class-repo/syllabus to pull the real course calendar, rather than guessing dates: 23 sessions, Tue/Thu 9:30 to 11:00 in room 33-218, with no class on Tue 13 Oct (MIT Monday schedule) or Thu 26 Nov (Thanksgiving).',
      'Built src/pages/schedule.astro: 14 weeks, each week listing its course sessions alongside two parallel task tracks, a course track and an ORLO* research track.',
      'Used the published Block A dates and topics for sessions 1 through 8 verbatim. Block B and C dates, and the Assignment 2 to 4 due dates, are labeled projected on the page because the syllabus says exact dates are posted once the roster settles and that assignments are released as the term goes.',
      'Carried the fixed deadlines onto the page: Assignment 1 Tue 15 Sep, one-pager Tue 22 Sep, lightning talks Thu 24 Sep, portfolio freeze Fri 4 Dec 5 pm, reflective one-on-ones week of 7 Dec.',
      'Sized the research track so each week is completable with an agent doing the drafting and sweeps, and attached a verification step to every week that produces a number: hand-check the R2 to R1 transform math, overfit a 16-image batch before any real training run, golden-file tests on the eval harness, independent recompute of one reported metric.',
      'Password protected the page with real encryption instead of a hidden div. The schedule HTML is rendered at build time, encrypted with AES-256-GCM using a key derived by PBKDF2-SHA256 at 150000 iterations, and only the ciphertext ships in the page. The browser decrypts with Web Crypto after the passphrase is entered.',
      'Confirmed the protection by searching the built dist/schedule/index.html for content terms: docking, keypoint, PyBullet, Thanksgiving, and 33-218 are all absent from the plaintext. Only the intentional public intro blurb is readable in source.',
      'Tested the gate in a real browser with Playwright: a wrong passphrase shows an error and leaves the content hidden, the passphrase Schedule unlocks all 14 weeks, the unlock survives a page reload via sessionStorage, and the lock again button re-hides the content. No console errors.',
      'Added Schedule to the nav in src/components/Header.astro.',
      'Rebuilt src/pages/project.astro from the submitted proposal, replacing the one-line placeholder: R1 and R2 architecture cards, the model-size versus positioning-accuracy research question, the R2-docks-with-R1 initial scope called out separately, the four evaluation metrics, the role of AI agents, and the three deliverables.',
      'Published Kuhl_Project_Proposal.pdf to public/docs/ and linked it twice from the project page, with a footer note stating the PDF is the submitted version of record so the reformatted prose is not mistaken for the submission.',
      'Removed the stale claim that the project direction was still being decided from both src/pages/index.astro and src/pages/about.astro, since it contradicted a settled proposal.',
      'Verified on the deployed site: /project and /schedule return 200, the proposal PDF returns 200 as application/pdf, the Term schedule link resolves correctly under the /michaelkuhl10 base path, the passphrase gate still works in production, and neither page overflows horizontally at 390 px.',
      'Left the earlier draft Robotic Control ORLO_ Proposal.txt untracked, since it contains advisor-facing open questions and candid self-assessment rather than public project description.',
    ],
    personalNotes: `I built out an internal schedule and updated the project page based on the submitted proposal.  Opus 5 produced the result.`,
  },
  {
    date: '2026-09-22',
    title: 'Built three pi skills for the ORLO* vision project',
    aiSummary: [
      'Read the project proposal text file (Robotic Control ORLO_ Proposal.txt) and the repo conventions, then scoped three skills against the proposal\'s stated deliverables: a reproducible simulation, a deployable model, and a quantitative trade-off study.',
      'Built docking-sim-scenarios (global skill): expands a sweep config into deterministic scenarios with ground-truth pose labels, per-sample seeds, and a manifest recording the config hash and scenario checksum.',
      'Generated 1944 scenarios (243 condition cells x 8 samples) from the example sweep, rendered all 1944 placeholder images in 16 seconds, and confirmed byte-identical regeneration from the manifest alone.',
      'Verified the dataset guardrails by deliberately breaking them: a hand-edited manifest triggers a config hash mismatch, a deleted scenario line triggers an integrity failure, and an unrendered dataset is refused by the benchmark.',
      'Built vision-model-benchmark (global skill): runs a model against a pinned dataset and records translation and rotation error at p50 and p95, latency at p50 and p95, peak memory, parameter count, docking success rate, abstention rate, and overconfident error rate.',
      'Included four models: oracle and null as sanity checks, classical_blob as the classical CV baseline, and noisy_oracle as a labeled synthetic placeholder.',
      'The oracle sanity model caught a real bug in the metric code: the standard 2*acos(|dot|) formula for quaternion rotation error reported 0.005 deg on an exact match because acos is ill-conditioned near dot=1. Replaced it with the atan2 form, which now returns exactly 0 and recovers a 0.001 deg rotation faithfully.',
      'Benchmarked all four models on the 1944-scenario dataset. classical_blob scored 0.0638 docking success against the null baseline\'s 0.2984, so the report now flags automatically when a model fails to beat null or is confidently wrong on more than 10 percent of scenarios.',
      'Per-condition breakdown showed classical_blob at 0 percent success under 5 degree grazing light versus 19 percent at 75 degrees, which is the low-light failure mode the space imaging notes predicted.',
      'Built dev-log-entry (project skill in .pi/skills/): inserts an entry at the top of the entries array, escapes single-quoted aiSummary strings and template-literal personalNotes, copies PDF attachments into public/docs/, rejects hrefs containing the base path, and verifies with npm run build.',
      'Tested dev-log-entry against adversarial input containing apostrophes, double quotes, a backtick, and a ${expr} sequence, confirmed the build passed and the rendered HTML preserved the text literally, then reverted the test entry.',
      'Restored public/docs/Kuhl_Project_Proposal.pdf, which was tracked in git but missing from the working tree while project.astro linked to it in two places.',
      'This entry was added using the dev-log-entry skill.',
    ],
    personalNotes: `I delivered my project proposal as a .txt in my local directory for Claude Opus 5 to read.  I had it come up with three skills that would be usefull as I progress in this project.  It developed docking simulation scenarios, a vision-model benchmark, and an update dev log skill which I am using now.  I still have to explore the skills to learn how to best use them.`,
  },
  {
    date: '2026-09-21',
    title: 'The Sept 21 Tragedy: pi loses its hands',
    attachment: {
      href: '/docs/Sept_21_Tragedy.pdf',
      label: 'Sept 21 Tragedy (PDF)',
    },
    aiSummary: [
      'Pi (the coding-agent CLI) auto-updated itself, changing how it hands tools and the system prompt to provider extensions: instead of passing a simple tool list and system prompt, it now bundles both into the conversation history and expects the provider to pull them back out with helper functions.',
      'The MIT Parley extension was written for the old, simpler approach, so every request it sent went out with no tools and, for most models, no system prompt — the AI silently pretended to read and write files while doing nothing, without ever erroring out.',
      'Fix: patched the extension’s request-building code (dist/stream.js) to fetch tools and the system prompt via pi’s new helper functions (getCurrentTools, getInitialSystemMessage) instead of reading the old fields directly.',
      'Verified with live tests that file reads, writes, and commands now actually execute.',
      'The underlying bug lives in the mit-parley-pi-extension npm package (maintainer: prash; repo: git@github.mit.edu:prash/pi_parley.git) and needs the same fix applied to src/stream.ts, then a new npm version published so it survives future pi updates.',
    ],
    personalNotes: `I spent 45 minutes tonight developing my own skills, but ran into an issue with pi when I tried to write these to .pi/Agent/Skills.  Pi had lost all ability to utilize its tools.  I had assumed this was the result of irresponsibly executing code in my terminal.  I spent 1.5 hours on my own trying to solve the problem and 2 more hours with my roomate who spends all day in terminal, and neither of us could figure out the issue.  We even executed the forbidden rm rf command to completely get rid of pi and re-install.  It was useless.  It was not until I connected Claude in terminal that we (Claude) were able to diagnose the issue.  I (Claude) provided the summary of the issue and the solution to it on the left.`,
  },
  {
    date: '2026-09-16',
    title: 'Assignment: pull session stats from pi session log',
    aiSummary: [
      'Pulled the pi coding-agent session log for the 2026-09-14 "Layout and styling fixes" session from ~/.pi/agent/sessions.',
      'Parsed the session\'s .jsonl file to count user-typed messages (4) and agent tool calls (141) for that session.',
      'Extracted the text of all 4 prompts typed during that session:',
      '1. "The website formatting is such that the JARVIS easter egg is hidden behind the navigation bar at the top. Also decrease emm dashes and be more succinct with communication."',
      '2. "move the made by jarvis piece appear to the right instead of on top. the writing on top of the project tab is covered up as well. Adjust padding to correct this and the jarvis thing. there are still em dashes in the project tab"',
      '3. "push and confirm. The link does not show the updated website"',
      '4. "the jarvis tab is not fixed. When I hover over it, it appears above the tessaract thing. This might be becuase the title is now on 2 lines instead of one which makes the \'made by jarvis\' tab be hiddden behind the top bar. I want everything to be visible when the website view is at 100%"',
    ],
    personalNotes: `I used Claude Sonnet 5 to understand how to execute the assignment.

I then had it pull the session details from the session that used the
most number of tools, because I was curious.

It seemed readjusting sizing and padding took more tool calling than
building the website in the first place... curious.

I remember this session took quite a long time for each prompt to
render a result.`,
  },
  {
    date: '2026-09-15',
    title: 'Simplified project scope while direction is finalized',
    aiSummary: [
      'Reduced the Project page to a single description: "AI agent to accelerate in-space assembly engineering research."',
      'Removed the AI-drafted problem statement, milestones, and success metrics from the Project page pending a settled project direction.',
      'Removed AI-drafted publication and test-result claims from site content files.',
      'Rebuilt the dev log to use plain, fact-based bullet points instead of narrative prose.',
      'Set up a separate, private notes location (markdown files, untracked) for personal reflections and AI-interaction notes.',
    ],
    personalNotes: `Add your own notes for this session here.`,
  },
  {
    date: '2026-09-14',
    title: 'Layout and styling fixes',
    aiSummary: [
      'Fixed a bug where the fixed header overlapped page content.',
      'Repositioned the hologram easter-egg element on the home page.',
      'Unified rounded-corner styling and button style across pages.',
      'Removed a placeholder diagram from the Project page.',
      'Removed an inaccurate personal-background detail from the About page.',
    ],
    personalNotes: `When revisiting the website on a different device, I noticed some of the padding and organization on the website was off - struggled for a bit to correct it`,
  },
  {
    date: '2026-09-11',
    title: 'Initial site build',
    aiSummary: [
      'Scaffolded an Astro + Tailwind CSS static site with a GitHub Actions deploy workflow to GitHub Pages.',
      'Built the page structure: Home, About, Project, Dev Log.',
      'Added personal photos and a short bio to the About page.',
      'Added a header, footer, and base layout shared across pages.',
      'Added an animated arc-reactor element and hover easter egg to the home page.',
      'Drafted an initial project one-pager and dev log template (later revised — see 2026-09-15).',
    ],
    personalNotes: `Jumped between models to explore cost and performance. Settled on Haiku and Sonnet as Opus was too expensive for the simplicity of the tasks performed. Used a combination of Claude Co-Work and Claude Code.  Used Co-Work to go through previously created files and come up with context that could be fed to Claude Code as it built the website.  I also used Co-Work to explore research directions and help scope something ambitious for this class. I had issues with misunderstanding context when using Haiku.  Switched over to Sonnet, and performance was better. Still have an error with the outlining of some text on the title page.`,
  },
];
---

<BaseLayout title="Dev Log">
  <section class="max-w-5xl mx-auto px-6 pt-12 pb-16">
    <h1 data-reveal="1">Dev Log</h1>
    <p class="mt-6 max-w-2xl text-base text-space-200" data-reveal="2">
      A plain, fact-based record of what was built or changed each session,
      paired with my own notes on that same work. The left column is the
      objective summary; the right column is mine — written by me, not
      AI-generated.
    </p>

    <div class="mt-12 space-y-12">
      {entries.map((entry, i) => (
        <article
          class="relative border-l-2 border-orbit/30 pl-6"
          data-reveal={String(Math.min(i + 1, 5))}
        >
          <span class="absolute -left-[7px] top-1.5 w-3 h-3 rounded-full bg-orbit shadow-orbit-sm"></span>
          <time class="font-mono text-xs text-space-400 tracking-widest">
            {entry.date}
          </time>
          <h3 class="mt-1 mb-4 text-orbit-light">{entry.title}</h3>

          <div class="grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-0 rounded-xl border border-orbit/15 overflow-hidden">
            <div class="p-5 border-b md:border-b-0 md:border-r border-orbit/15 bg-space-900/30">
              <p class="font-mono text-xs text-orbit tracking-widest mb-3">SESSION SUMMARY</p>
              <ul class="text-base text-space-300 m-0 space-y-1 list-disc pl-5">
                {entry.aiSummary.map((bullet) => (
                  <li>{bullet}</li>
                ))}
              </ul>
              {entry.attachment && (
                <a
                  href={resolvePath(entry.attachment.href)}
                  target="_blank"
                  rel="noopener"
                  class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 mt-4 text-sm text-orbit-light hover:text-orbit underline"
                >
                  ↓ {entry.attachment.label}
                </a>
              )}
            </div>
            <div class="p-5 bg-thruster-500/[0.04]">
              <p class="font-mono text-xs text-thruster-400 tracking-widest mb-3">MY NOTES</p>
              <p class="text-base text-space-300 m-0 whitespace-pre-line">
                {entry.personalNotes}
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </article>
      ))}
    </div>

    <p class="mt-12 text-sm text-space-400 border-t border-orbit/10 pt-6">
      AI session summaries are on the left, and personal reflections are on the right.
    </p>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
